ngths. Hard.\n\nBut maybe easier: Use vector geometry with complex numbers.\n\nComplex numbers: Let circumcircle be unit circle, points a,b,c on unit circle. Then the tangent at a is line {z: (z-a) \\overline{a} + \\overline{z} a =0}? Actually condition for tangency: (z-a) \\overline{a} + \\overline{z} a =0 => \\overline{a} z + a \\overline{z} = 2 Re(a \\overline{z}) = 2? Since |a|=1, the tangent line is {z: Re(a \\overline{z}) = 1}. Similarly for b. Intersection S solves Re(a \\overline{z}) = Re(b \\overline{z}) = 1. Subtract: Re((a-b) \\overline{z}) =0 => (a-b) \\overline{z} is purely imaginary. So \\overline{z} is orthogonal to a-b in real sense. This yields that z is some value.\n\nBut maybe we can find S explicitly: Solve for z satisfying Re(a \\overline{z}) = 1 and Re(b \\overline{z}) = 1. Subtract: Re((a-b) \\overline{z}) =0 => (a-b) \\overline{z} is pure imaginary => \\overline{z} is orthogonal to a-b. So \\overline{z} is some multiple of i (a-b). So \\overline{z}= i (a-b) t for some real t. Plug into first equation: Re(a \\overline{z}) = Re(a i (a-b) t) = t Re(i a (a-b)). Since a is unit, a (a-b) = a^2 - a b. Multiply by i: i a^2 - i a b. Real part of that is? Write a = e^{i\u03b1}, b = e^{i\u03b2}. Then a^2 = e^{i2\u03b1}, a b = e^{i(\u03b1+\u03b2)}. Then i a^2 = i e^{i2\u03b1} = e^{i(2\u03b1+\u03c0/2)}. Real part = cos(2\u03b1+\u03c0/2) = -sin 2\u03b1. Similarly i a b = i e^{i(\u03b1+\u03b2)} => real part = -sin(\u03b1+\u03b2). So Re(i a (a-b)) = -sin 2\u03b1 + sin(\u03b1+\u03b2). Not simple.\n\nBut maybe we can find S as the point with argument equal to (\u03b1+\u03b2)/2? Actually the line through O perpendicular to AB is the line through origin with direction vector (b-a) rotated 90 degrees? The pole of AB is the intersection of tangents, which lies on the line through O perpendicular to AB. So S is somewhere on that line.\n\nNow H' is reflection of H across AB. Since AB is horizontal in our coordinate, H' is symmetric vertically. The line through S and